 "life": {
  "note": "Coral cay profile for web/reef/life.js (format in web/reef/life/profile.js). Plants: Wikipedia, Green Island National Park: the island is covered in tropical vine forest; Wikipedia, Green Island (Queensland): dry coastal/beach plants and a vine thicket rain forest. No species list of the island's plants could be read (the QPWS management plan was not reachable), so they are drawn as 'vine-forest' and 'beach-scrub', without species. Turtles: green and hawksbill turtles are seen offshore (Wikipedia, Green Island National Park); sea turtles feed on the seagrass in the shallows (greatadventures.com.au/about-green-island): green turtles 2.5 and hawksbills 1.5 times as common as by default (multipliers not sourced). Spawning: an offshore reef (Willis et al. 1985 (researchonline.jcu.edu.au/24208): the offshore mass spawning period is one lunar month after the inshore one; Peter Harrison in Australian Geographic, 'Synchronised sex', 9 May 2016: the periods after the full moon in October and November for the inshore reefs, and November and December for the offshore and northern and southern reefs), lag 1.",
